  "ruleDraft": {
    "status": "draft_only_not_implemented",
    "why_not_a_callSiteAdaptation": "There is no wrong call to rewrite: 1.20.1 code never calls a registry-key setter, so a call-site rule has nothing to match. The key has to be injected between 'new Settings()' and 'new Item(settings)', using an id that is usually a separate static field read at the register call.",
    "why_not_a_constructorRedirect": "constructorRedirects replace one constructor call with a factory that receives the same arguments. The registry key is not among those arguments; it has to be recovered from the registry id passed to a later call in the same method.",
    "proposed_shape": {
      "kind": "registrationSiteRewrite",
      "match": {
        "registerCall": "class_2378.method_10230(registry, id, value)",
        "valueConstruction": "value is a fresh instance whose constructor took a Settings built in the same method"
      },
      "rewrite": "emit bridge call RegistrationKeyBridge.register(registry, id, supplier) where the supplier builds the value with settings.method_63686(RegistryKey.of(RegistryKeys.ITEM, id))",
      "requires": [
        "a bridge that accepts a factory instead of an instance",
        "identity of the registry (item/block/entity) to pick RegistryKeys.*",
        "a decision for values that are not constructed inline (field holders)"
      ],
      "prerequisite": "A bytecode data-flow pass that ties the register call to the construction it registers; the current rule engine matches single call sites."
    },
    "verification_plan": "Patch the fixture, then assert: the patched class calls method_63686 before class_1792.<init>; a 1.21.4 boot does not raise 'Item id not set'. Only the first half is possible without a game instance."
  },
  "passed": true,
  "scope": "measurement only. Nothing was converted, no rule was written, no game was launched: this locates the shape of the gap, it does not close it."
